<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andrew II of Hungary</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1177–<a href="/years/1235">1235</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Andrew II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andrew II was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> from 1205, of the Árpád dynasty. He led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifth-crusade">Fifth Crusade</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> in 1217–1218 and spent heavily on wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, granting away royal estates to his supporters. Pressure from the lesser nobility forced him to issue the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1222">Golden Bull of 1222</a>, which limited royal power. He was the father of Béla IV and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-of-hungary">Saint Elizabeth of Hungary</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andrew-ii-of-hungary.jpg" alt="Andrew II of Hungary" width="320" height="339" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: György Klösz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andrew_II_seal_1224.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…as frontier warriors, Muslim and Jewish merchants handled trade and sometimes royal finances, and from the twelfth century Germans, Walloons and Italians were invited as ‘guests’ (hospites) with their own liberties; Géza II settled the Saxons of Transylvania, whose rights <b>Andrew II</b> confirmed in the Andreanum of 1224.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After King <b>Andrew II of Hungary</b> expelled the knights from the Burzenland in Transylvania in 1225, Duke Konrad I of Masovia invited them to help him against the pagan Prussians who were raiding his lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>King <b>Andrew II's</b> son Béla set up the banate around 1228–1233, in lands taken from Bulgaria and the Cumans east of the Iron Gates, around the fortress of Severin (Drobeta-Turnu Severin) built on Roman ruins.</blockquote><a href="/state/banate-of-severin">Banate of Severin</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.47,19.05&amp;year=1214" aria-label="Buda on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4995 499 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Andrew II of Hungary"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5445 769h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5445 769h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=47.47,19.05&amp;year=1214">What was here in 1214? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
